{
  "case_id": "2020-20-440",
  "document_hash": "c71ac17a6eb4980d91f6b7b7af1e968cc7442eb0e7e16efcb897511561d27cd4",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Minerva Surgical, Inc.",
      "case_id": "2020-20-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c71ac17a6eb4980d91f6b7b7af1e968cc7442eb0e7e16efcb897511561d27cd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c71ac17a6eb4-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Patent Act doesn't provide for assignor estoppel and never has. In fact, it says invalidity shall be a defense in any action. That's essential to the fundamental patent market. The public grants exclusive rights but only to the extent inventors publicly share useful advances in knowledge. Accused infringers who prove a patent is invalid vindicate the right of all to make and use and sell unpatented project -- products."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Hologic, Inc.",
      "case_id": "2020-20-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c71ac17a6eb4980d91f6b7b7af1e968cc7442eb0e7e16efcb897511561d27cd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c71ac17a6eb4-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Hologic says Congress didn't have to write assignor estoppel into the Patent Act. It reads this Court's 1924 decision in Formica as having settled assignor estoppel into patent law. We don't think that's what Formica did,"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-20-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c71ac17a6eb4980d91f6b7b7af1e968cc7442eb0e7e16efcb897511561d27cd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c71ac17a6eb4-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Yes, thank you, Mr. Chief Justice. Counsel, you said that the -- you could not compare assignor estoppel to issue -- concepts such as issue preclusion or claim preclusion, et cetera. You -- you distinguished them, but I don't think you demonstrated why those principles, which do not appear in the Patent Act, are applicable or acceptable, but assignor estoppel is not."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2020-20-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c71ac17a6eb4980d91f6b7b7af1e968cc7442eb0e7e16efcb897511561d27cd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c71ac17a6eb4-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Well, you're just quibbling with my assumption, because my assumption was that Scott Paper created an exception to it, left the rule in place. So what are your --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2020-20-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c71ac17a6eb4980d91f6b7b7af1e968cc7442eb0e7e16efcb897511561d27cd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c71ac17a6eb4-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: Let me come at the problem a different way. It -- it seems to me that we all agree that the common law would have had an equitable estoppel defense here available. And you don't contest that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2020-20-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c71ac17a6eb4980d91f6b7b7af1e968cc7442eb0e7e16efcb897511561d27cd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c71ac17a6eb4-0001-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. RATNER: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: As Petitioner has explained, the Federal Circuit's test for assignor estoppel is too broad. That court prevents an assignor from challenging any claim relating to an assigned invention, even if that claim looks nothing like the claims that existed at the time of the assignment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2020-20-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c71ac17a6eb4980d91f6b7b7af1e968cc7442eb0e7e16efcb897511561d27cd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c71ac17a6eb4-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: And how are you solving that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Ratner",
      "case_id": "2020-20-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c71ac17a6eb4980d91f6b7b7af1e968cc7442eb0e7e16efcb897511561d27cd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c71ac17a6eb4-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. RATNER: No, Justice Alito. We do think it is -- is good policy, but we also think that it derives both from this Court's decision in Westinghouse and, before that, from basic principles of estoppel by deed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2020-20-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c71ac17a6eb4980d91f6b7b7af1e968cc7442eb0e7e16efcb897511561d27cd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c71ac17a6eb4-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: If you would think about the second prong of your test, what decision of a federal court has applied that prong?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Ratner",
      "case_id": "2020-20-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c71ac17a6eb4980d91f6b7b7af1e968cc7442eb0e7e16efcb897511561d27cd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c71ac17a6eb4-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. RATNER: So there isn't a decision. This is the question that the Court left open in Westinghouse."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Wolf",
      "case_id": "2020-20-440",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "c71ac17a6eb4980d91f6b7b7af1e968cc7442eb0e7e16efcb897511561d27cd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c71ac17a6eb4-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "in Scott Paper, this Court considered whether or not to reverse Westinghouse and expressly said it was not doing so. Rather, it created a narrow exception based on this Court's long-held concerns about temporal expansions of patent monopolies."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2020-20-440",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "c71ac17a6eb4980d91f6b7b7af1e968cc7442eb0e7e16efcb897511561d27cd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c71ac17a6eb4-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I'd -- I'd like to see if there's a difference between your position and that of the Solicitor -- Solicitor General, in particular, for the person who enters employment and signs a general assignment of all her inventions to her employer. Does equitable or assignor estoppel apply in that case?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-20-440",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "c71ac17a6eb4980d91f6b7b7af1e968cc7442eb0e7e16efcb897511561d27cd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c71ac17a6eb4-0003-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice. Counsel, it seems as though your view of assignor estoppel begins to approach the assignments that one would require from an employee. It seems -- so how far would you go from the original assignment? Would you -- in this case, in the current case, the -- we're talking about a patent that is quite different from the original patent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. WOLF",
      "case_id": "2020-20-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c71ac17a6eb4980d91f6b7b7af1e968cc7442eb0e7e16efcb897511561d27cd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c71ac17a6eb4-0004-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 85,
        "start": 85
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Your -- Your Honor, prior\nto 1952, we do not believe there's any\nuncertainty. Westinghouse said it was\nmanifestly intended by Congress. Scott said\nexpressly and explicitly it was not overturning\nthe doctrine."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE BARRETT",
      "case_id": "2020-20-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c71ac17a6eb4980d91f6b7b7af1e968cc7442eb0e7e16efcb897511561d27cd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c71ac17a6eb4-0004-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 85,
        "start": 85
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But, counsel, the --\nthe -- the language -- and, you know, this has\ncome up already -- I mean, when you have the\nlanguage in Scott Paper and then in Lear saying\nthat Scott Paper undermined any basis for\nassignor estoppel, I mean, you can't say that it\nwas completely embraced."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. HOCHMAN",
      "case_id": "2020-20-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c71ac17a6eb4980d91f6b7b7af1e968cc7442eb0e7e16efcb897511561d27cd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c71ac17a6eb4-0004-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 89,
        "start": 89
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "He -- he -- he said repeatedly that\nClaim 31 was obtained. That's not true. Claim\n31 was canceled and it was canceled two years,\ntwo full years before Cytyc lost the patent. So\nit was not -- and -- and -- and that's just the\nway the patent prosecution process goes."
    },
    {
